            <ArticleTitle>Do We Need Extra-Articular Reconstructive Surgery?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>61-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0278-5919(17)30075-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.csm.2017.07.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>With renewed interest in the lateral soft tissue envelope anatomy, there is also a rise in the popularity of extra-articular anterolateral procedures. There is reasonable laboratory-based evidence for additional benefit of such procedures, but clinical data are not sufficient to judge outcome in the long term for better or worse. Furthermore, the decision-making process to decide when to add an extra-articular procedure is lacking; there are no clinical tests or investigations to guide the clinician. This article presents an overview of the literature and reflections from the authors on the subject.</AbstractText>
